db/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420688 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186420670 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420688 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420688 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420688 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186420635 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420688 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "ecfcee5e-24eb-56dc-ac84-c2b7a4109c97" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420689 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186420680 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420689 #edb/ref 1069 "q-216443" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420689 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420689 #edb/ref 1071 "$\\,$ ${n}^{2}+7n+12=0$\n\nWhich of the following conditions are sufficient for the above to be true?\n\n- $n\\ge -4$\n- $n=-4$\n- $|n|\\le 4$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420689 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "00b6d002-6ca8-5b5f-a710-55bc4f02fb94" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420689 #edb/ref 1074 "Given that the implication $S⇒N$ is true, we have the following:\n\n- $S$ being true *guarantees* that $N$ must also be true. Therefore, $S$ is a *sufficient condition* for $N.$\n\n- For $S$ to be true, it is *necessary* that $N$ is true. Therefore, $N$ is a *necessary condition* for $S.$ However, note that $N$ being true does *not* guarantee that $S$ is true.\n\nThese ideas can be summarized as follows:\n\n$$\n\\text{S}\\text{ufficient}\\,⇒\\,\\text{N}\\text{ecessary}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nIn our case, we need to find the conditions that are *sufficient* for the given one:\n\n$$\n\\text{S}\\text{ufficient}\\,⇒\\,({n}^{2}+7n+12=0)\n$$\n\nNote that the solutions to the given equation are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}^{2}+7n+12 & =0 \\\\ (n+4)(n+3) & =0 \\\\ n & =-4,-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given conditions.\n\n- Condition I is *not* a sufficient condition:\n\n$$\n(n\\ge -4)\\,⇏\\,({n}^{2}+7n+12=0)\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nFor example, if $n=0,$ then $n\\ge -4,$ but ${n}^{2}+7n+12\\ne 0.$\n\n- Condition II is a sufficient condition:\n\n$$\n(n=-4)\\,⇒\\,({n}^{2}+7n+12=0)\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\nIf $n=-4,$ then ${n}^{2}+7n+12=0.$\n\n- Condition III is *not* a sufficient condition:\n\n$$\n(|n|\\le 4)\\,⇏\\,({n}^{2}+7n+12=0)\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nFor example, if $n=0,$ then $|n|\\le 4,$ but ${n}^{2}+7n+12\\ne 0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420690 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186420681 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420690 #edb/ref 1069 "q-172300" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420690 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420690 #edb/ref 1071 "$\\,$ ${n}^{2}$ is an even number\n\nWhich of the following conditions are necessary for the above to be true?\n\n- $n$ is an odd number\n- $n$ is a positive number\n- $n$ is a multiple of $2$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420690 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "04618c03-3ca0-590d-b082-33d1176f03c4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420690 #edb/ref 1074 "Given that the implication $S⇒N$ is true, we have the following:\n\n- $S$ being true *guarantees* that $N$ must also be true. Therefore, $S$ is a *sufficient condition* for $N.$\n\n- For $S$ to be true, it is *necessary* that $N$ is true. Therefore, $N$ is a *necessary condition* for $S.$ However, note that $N$ being true does *not* guarantee that $S$ is true.\n\nThese ideas can be summarized as follows:\n\n$$\n\\text{S}\\text{ufficient}\\,⇒\\,\\text{N}\\text{ecessary}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we need to find the conditions that are *necessary* from the given one:\n\n$$\n({n}^{2}\\text{ is an even number})\\,⇒\\,\\text{N}\\text{ecessary}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given conditions. \n\n- Condition I is *not* a necessary condition: \n\n$$\n({n}^{2}\\text{ is an even number })\\,⇏\\,(n\\text{ is an odd number})\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nFor example, the number $n=2$ is not an odd number but ${2}^{2}$ is an even number.\n\n- Condition II is *not* a necessary condition: \n\n$$\n({n}^{2}\\text{ is an even number})\\,⇏\\,(\\text{ is a positive number})\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nFor example, the number $n=-2$ is not a positive number, but $(-2)^{2}$ is an even number.\n\n- Condition III is a necessary condition: \n\n$$\n({n}^{2}\\text{ is an even number})\\,⇒\\,(n\\text{ is a multiple of }2)\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\nSince ${n}^{2}$ is even, it must also be true that $n$ is even. Therefore, $n$ is a multiple of $2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420691 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186420682 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420691 #edb/ref 1069 "q-172315" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420691 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420691 #edb/ref 1071 "$\\,$ ${n}^{2}=16$\n\nWhich of the following conditions are sufficient for the above to be true?\n\n- $n=4$\n- $-4<n<4$\n- $|n|<16$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420691 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "10fd92b2-5e05-5850-b7d2-d8140b9fd04f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420691 #edb/ref 1074 "Given that the implication $S⇒N$ is true, we have the following:\n\n- $S$ being true *guarantees* that $N$ must also be true. Therefore, $S$ is a *sufficient condition* for $N.$\n\n- For $S$ to be true, it is *necessary* that $N$ is true. Therefore, $N$ is a *necessary condition* for $S.$ However, note that $N$ being true does *not* guarantee that $S$ is true.\n\nThese ideas can be summarized as follows:\n\n$$\n\\text{S}\\text{ufficient}\\,⇒\\,\\text{N}\\text{ecessary}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we need to find the conditions that are *sufficient* for the given one:\n\n$$\n\\text{S}\\text{ufficient}\\,⇒\\,({n}^{2}=16)\n$$\n\nNote that the solution to the given equation is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}^{2} & =16 \\\\ {n}^{2}-16 & =0 \\\\ (n+4)(n-4) & =0 \\\\ n & =-4,4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given conditions.\n\n- Condition I is a sufficient condition:\n\n$$\n(n=4)\\,⇒\\,({n}^{2}=16)\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\nIf $n=4,$ then ${n}^{2}=16.$\n\n- Condition II is *not* a sufficient condition:\n\n$$\n(-4<n<4)\\,⇏\\,({n}^{2}=16)\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nFor example, if $n=-1,$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}^{2} & =(-1)^{2} \\\\  & =1\\ne 16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Condition III is *not* a sufficient condition:\n\n$$\n(|n|<16)\\,⇏\\,({n}^{2}=16)\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nFor example, if $n=-1,$ then $|n|<16,$ but ${n}^{2}=1\\ne 16.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420692 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186420672 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420692 #edb/ref 1069 "q-172325" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420692 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420692 #edb/ref 1071 "$\\,$ $x+1>4$\n\nWhich of the following conditions are sufficient for the above to be true?\n\n- $|x|>3$\n- $x>3$\n- $x>4$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420692 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "28567992-ba7f-5623-ad07-13abe4be416c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420692 #edb/ref 1074 "Given that the implication $S⇒N$ is true, we have the following:\n\n- $S$ being true *guarantees* that $N$ must also be true. Therefore, $S$ is a *sufficient condition* for $N.$\n\n- For $S$ to be true, it is *necessary* that $N$ is true. Therefore, $N$ is a *necessary condition* for $S.$ However, note that $N$ being true does *not* guarantee that $S$ is true.\n\nThese ideas can be summarized as follows:\n\n$$\n\\text{S}\\text{ufficient}\\,⇒\\,\\text{N}\\text{ecessary}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nIn our case, we need to find the conditions that are *sufficient* for the given one:\n\n$$\n\\text{S}\\text{ufficient}\\,⇒\\,(x+1>4)\n$$\n\nNote that the solution to the given equation is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+1 & >4 \\\\ x & >3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given conditions.\n\n- Condition I is *not* a sufficient condition:\n\n$$\n(|x|>3)\\,⇏\\,(x+1>4)\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nFor example, if $x=-4,$ then we have $x+1=-3≯4.$\n\n- Condition II is a sufficient condition:\n\n$$\n(x>3)\\,⇒\\,(x+1>4)\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\nIf $x>3,$ then $x+1>4.$\n\n- Condition III is a sufficient condition:\n\n$$\n(x>4)\\,⇒\\,(x+1>4)\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\nIf $x>4,$ then  $x+1>5>4.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420693 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186420678 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420693 #edb/ref 1069 "q-216459" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420693 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420693 #edb/ref 1071 "$\\,$ ${f}^{′}(x)={sec}^{2}⁡x$\n\nWhich of the following conditions are necessary and sufficient for the above statement to be true?\n\n- $f$ is a differentiable function\n- $f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$\n- $f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+C,$ for any $C\\in R$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420693 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "390cc44c-87e5-5905-81de-8b4495a342fd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420693 #edb/ref 1074 "Given that the equivalence $P⇔Q$ is true, we have that $P$ is a *necessary and sufficient condition* for $Q.$  \n\nIn our case, we need to find the conditions that are *necessary and sufficient* for the given one. In other words,\n\n$$\n({f}^{′}(x)={sec}^{2}⁡x)\\,⇒\\,\\text{Condition}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n({f}^{′}(x)={sec}^{2}⁡x)\\,⇐\\,\\text{Condition}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the solution to the differential equation ${f}^{′}(x)={sec}^{2}⁡x$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\int {sec}^{2}⁡x\\,dx \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C\\in R$ is the arbitrary constant.\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given conditions.\n\n- Condition I is *not* a sufficient condition:\n\n$$\n({f}^{′}(x)={sec}^{2}⁡x)\\,⇍\\,(f\\text{ is a differentiable function})\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nFor example, the function $f(x)={e}^{x}$ is a differentiable function but ${f}^{′}(x)\\ne {sec}^{2}⁡x.$\n\n- Condition II is *not* a necessary condition:\n\n$$\n({f}^{′}(x)={sec}^{2}⁡x)\\,⇏\\,(f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nFor example, for the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+1,$ the statement ${f}^{′}(x)={sec}^{2}⁡x$ is true but $f(x)\\ne \\operatorname{tan}⁡x+1.$\n\n- Condition III is both necessary and sufficient: The statement ${f}^{′}(x)={sec}^{2}⁡x$ is true *if and only if* $f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+C,$ for any $C\\in R.$\n\n$$\n({f}^{′}(x)={sec}^{2}⁡x)\\,⇒\\,(f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+C)\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n({f}^{′}(x)={sec}^{2}⁡x)\\,⇐\\,(f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+C).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write\n\n$$\n({f}^{′}(x)={sec}^{2}⁡x)\\,⇔\\,(f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+C)\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420694 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186420673 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420694 #edb/ref 1069 "q-172302" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420694 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420694 #edb/ref 1071 "$\\,$ ${n}^{2}$ is an odd number\n\nWhich of the following conditions are necessary for the above to be true?\n\n- $n$ is odd\n- $n$ is a multiple of $3$\n- $n$ is a multiple of $5$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420694 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4b376045-26da-55cb-becc-db924f8d2dac" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420694 #edb/ref 1074 "Given that the implication $S⇒N$ is true, we have the following:\n\n- $S$ being true *guarantees* that $N$ must also be true. Therefore, $S$ is a *sufficient condition* for $N.$\n\n- For $S$ to be true, it is *necessary* that $N$ is true. Therefore, $N$ is a *necessary condition* for $S.$ However, note that $N$ being true does *not* guarantee that $S$ is true.\n\nThese ideas can be summarized as follows:\n\n$$\n\\text{S}\\text{ufficient}\\,⇒\\,\\text{N}\\text{ecessary}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we need to find the conditions that are *necessary* from the given one:\n\n$$\n({n}^{2}\\text{ is an odd number})\\,⇒\\,\\text{N}\\text{ecessary}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given conditions. \n\n- Condition I is a necessary condition: \n\n$$\n({n}^{2}\\text{ is an odd number})\\,⇒\\,(\\text{ }n\\text{ is odd})\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\nSince ${n}^{2}$ is odd, it must also be true that $n$ is odd.\n\n- Condition II is *not* a necessary condition: \n\n$$\n({n}^{2}\\text{ is an odd number})\\,⇏\\,(n\\text{ is a multiple of }3)\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nFor example, the number $n=7$ is not a multiple of $3,$ but ${n}^{2}=49$ is an odd number.\n\n- Condition III is *not* a necessary condition: \n\n$$\n({n}^{2}\\text{ is an odd number})\\,⇏\\,(n\\text{ is a multiple of }5)\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nFor example, the number $n=9$ is not a multiple of $5,$ but ${n}^{2}=81$ is an odd number.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420695 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186420674 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420695 #edb/ref 1069 "q-183415" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420695 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420695 #edb/ref 1071 "$\\,$ $(m>0\\,∧n>0)⇒(m\\cdot n>0)$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the true implication above?\n\n- $m>0\\,∧n>0$ is a **necessary** condition for $m\\cdot n>0$\n- $m>0\\,∧n>0$ is a **sufficient** condition for $m\\cdot n>0$\n- $m\\cdot n>0$ is a **necessary** condition for $m>0\\,∧n>0$\n- $m\\cdot n>0$ is a **sufficient** condition for $m>0\\,∧n>0$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420695 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5116bec0-cc13-5aa6-83e8-d90bc6884457" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420695 #edb/ref 1074 "Given that the implication $S⇒N$ is true, we have the following:\n\n- $S$ being true *guarantees* that $N$ must also be true. Therefore, $S$ is a *sufficient condition* for $N.$\n\n- For $S$ to be true, it is *necessary* that $N$ is true. Therefore, $N$ is a *necessary condition* for $S.$ However, note that $N$ being true does *not* guarantee that $S$ is true.\n\nThese ideas can be summarized as follows:\n\n$$\n\\text{S}\\text{ufficient}\\,⇒\\,\\text{N}\\text{ecessary}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n{{(\\,m>0\\,∧n>0\\,)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{sufficient}}\\,⇒\\,{{(\\,m\\cdot n>0\\,)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{necessary}}\n$$\n\nis a true statement. Therefore:\n\n- Statement II is true. $m>0\\,∧n>0$ is a **sufficient** condition for $m\\cdot n>0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. $m\\cdot n>0$ is a **necessary** condition for $m>0\\,∧n>0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420696 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186420685 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420696 #edb/ref 1069 "q-216449" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420696 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420696 #edb/ref 1071 "$\\,$ $12∣x$\n\nWhich of the following conditions are necessary and sufficient for the above statement to be true?\n\n- $2∣x$ and $6∣x$\n- $3∣x$ and $4∣x$\n- $3∣x$ and $8∣x$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420696 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5904ff03-6479-54c0-a38b-31e73684000e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420696 #edb/ref 1074 "Given that the equivalence $P⇔Q$ is true, we have that $P$ is a *necessary and sufficient condition* for $Q.$  \n\nIn our case, we need to find the conditions that are *necessary and sufficient* for the given one. In other words,\n\n$$\n(12∣x)\\,⇒\\,\\text{Condition}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n(12∣x)\\,⇐\\,\\text{Condition}.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given conditions.\n\n- Condition I is *not* a sufficient condition:\n\n$$\n(12∣x)\\,⇍\\,(2∣x\\text{ and }6∣x)\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nFor example, the number $x=6$ is divisible by $2$ and by $6$ but not divisible by $12.$\n\n- Condition II is both necessary and sufficient: A number is divisible by $12$ *if and only if* it's divisible by both $3$ and $4.$\n\n$$\n(12∣x)\\,⇒\\,(3∣x\\text{ and }4∣x)\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n(12∣x)\\,⇐\\,(3∣x\\text{ and }4∣x).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write\n\n$$\n(12∣x)\\,⇔\\,(3∣x\\text{ and }4∣x)\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\n- Condition III is *not* a necessary condition:\n\n$$\n(12∣x)\\,⇏\\,(3∣x\\text{ and }8∣x)\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nFor example, the number $x=12$ is divisible by $12$ but not divisible by $8.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420697 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186420675 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420697 #edb/ref 1069 "q-183393" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420697 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420697 #edb/ref 1071 "$\\,$ If $l$ and $m$ are coplanar and perpendicular lines, then $l\\cap m\\ne ∅.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the implication above?\n\n- $l$ and $m$ being coplanar and perpendicular lines is a **necessary** condition for $l\\cap m\\ne ∅$\n- $l\\cap m\\ne ∅$ is a **necessary** condition for $l$ and $m$ being coplanar and perpendicular lines\n- $l$ and $m$ being coplanar and perpendicular lines is a **sufficient** condition for $l\\cap m\\ne ∅$\n- $l\\cap m\\ne ∅$ is a **sufficient** condition for $l$ and $m$ being coplanar and perpendicular lines" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420697 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5bbf3a4e-b9b9-5f74-9d97-2501c2b02398" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420697 #edb/ref 1074 "Given that the implication $S⇒N$ is true, we have the following:\n\n- $S$ being true *guarantees* that $N$ must also be true. Therefore, $S$ is a *sufficient condition* for $N.$\n\n- For $S$ to be true, it is *necessary* that $N$ is true. Therefore, $N$ is a *necessary condition* for $S.$ However, note that $N$ being true does *not* guarantee that $S$ is true.\n\nThese ideas can be summarized as follows:\n\n$$\n\\text{S}\\text{ufficient}\\,⇒\\,\\text{N}\\text{ecessary}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nIn our case, \n\n$$\n{{(\\,l,m\\text{ are coplanar and perpendicular lines}\\,)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{sufficient}}\\,⇒\\,{{(\\,l\\cap m\\ne ∅\\,)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{necessary}}\n$$\n\nis a true statement. Therefore:\n\n- Statement II is true. $l\\cap m\\ne ∅$ is a **necessary** condition for $l$ and $m$ being coplanar and perpendicular lines\n\n- Statement III is true. $l$ and $m$ being coplanar and perpendicular lines is a **sufficient** condition for $l\\cap m\\ne ∅$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420698 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186420679 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420698 #edb/ref 1069 "q-172298" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420698 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420698 #edb/ref 1071 "$\\,$ $|x-2|=6$\n\nWhich of the following conditions are necessary for the above to be true?\n\n- $x>4$\n- $|x|<10$\n- $x=8$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420698 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5ef2cacc-c405-55b2-8430-66b58129ad71" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420698 #edb/ref 1074 "Given that the implication $S⇒N$ is true, we have the following:\n\n- $S$ being true *guarantees* that $N$ must also be true. Therefore, $S$ is a *sufficient condition* for $N.$\n\n- For $S$ to be true, it is *necessary* that $N$ is true. Therefore, $N$ is a *necessary condition* for $S.$ However, note that $N$ being true does *not* guarantee that $S$ is true.\n\nThese ideas can be summarized as follows:\n\n$$\n\\text{S}\\text{ufficient}\\,⇒\\,\\text{N}\\text{ecessary}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nIn our case, we need to find the conditions that are *necessary* from the given one:\n\n$$\n(|x-2|=6)\\,⇒\\,\\text{N}\\text{ecessary}\n$$\n\nNote that the solution to the given equation is  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|x-2| & =6 \\\\ x-2 & =\\pm 6 \\\\ x & =-4,8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given conditions.\n\n- Condition I is *not* a necessary condition:\n\n$$\n(|x-2|=6)\\,⇏\\,(x>4)\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nFor example, if $x=-4,$ then $|x-2|=6,$ but $x≯4.$\n\n- Condition II is a necessary condition:\n\n$$\n(|x-2|=6)\\,⇒\\,(|x|<10)\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\nEach solution of $|x-2|=6$ satisfies $|x|<10.$\n\n- Condition III is *not* a necessary condition:\n\n$$\n(|x-2|=6)\\,⇏\\,(x=8)\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nFor example, if $x=-4,$ then $|x-2|=6,$ but $x\\ne 8.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420699 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186420684 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420699 #edb/ref 1069 "q-183429" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420699 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420699 #edb/ref 1071 "$\\,$ $20∣x$\n\nWhich of the following conditions are necessary and sufficient for the above statement to be true?\n\n- $2∣x$ and $10∣x$\n- $4∣x$ and $5∣x$\n- $100∣x$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420699 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8e1662ac-5299-5cc7-92ca-68567f121fc8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420699 #edb/ref 1074 "Given that the equivalence $P⇔Q$ is true, we have that $P$ is a *necessary and sufficient condition* for $Q.$  \n\nIn our case, we need to find the conditions that are *necessary and sufficient* for the given one. In other words,\n\n$$\n(20∣x)\\,⇒\\,\\text{Condition}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n(20∣x)\\,⇐\\,\\text{Condition}.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given conditions.\n\n- Condition I is *not* a sufficient condition:\n\n$$\n(20∣x)\\,⇍\\,(2∣x\\text{ and }10∣x)\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nFor example, the number $x=10$ is divisible by $2$ and by $10$ but not divisible by $20.$\n\n- Condition II is both necessary and sufficient: A number is divisible by $20$ *if and only if* it's divisible by both $4$ and $5.$\n\n$$\n(20∣x)\\,⇒\\,(4∣x\\text{ and }5∣x)\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n(20∣x)\\,⇐\\,(4∣x\\text{ and }5∣x).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write\n\n$$\n(20∣x)\\,⇔\\,(4∣x\\text{ and }5∣x)\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\n- Condition III is *not* a necessary condition:\n\n$$\n(20∣x)\\,⇏\\,(100∣x)\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nFor example, the number $x=20$ is divisible by $20$ but not divisible by $100.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420700 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186420687 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420700 #edb/ref 1069 "q-183485" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420700 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420700 #edb/ref 1071 "$\\,$ ${f}^{′}(x)=2x$\n\nWhich of the following conditions are necessary and sufficient for the above statement to be true?\n\n- ${f}^{″}(x)=2$\n- $f(x)={x}^{2}+C,$ for any $C\\in R$\n- $f(x)={x}^{2}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420700 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9dd0c0c2-7652-5690-ae78-53d067f574fa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420700 #edb/ref 1074 "Given that the equivalence $P⇔Q$ is true, we have that $P$ is a *necessary and sufficient condition* for $Q.$  \n\nIn our case, we need to find the conditions that are *necessary and sufficient* for the given one. In other words,\n\n$$\n({f}^{′}(x)=2x)\\,⇒\\,\\text{Condition}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n({f}^{′}(x)=2x)\\,⇐\\,\\text{Condition}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the solution to the differential equation ${f}^{′}(x)=2x$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\int 2x\\,dx \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C\\in R$ is the arbitrary constant.\n\nMoreover, the differential equation ${f}^{″}(x)=2$ gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\int 2\\,dx \\\\  & =2x+D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D\\in R$ is another arbitrary constant.\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given conditions.\n\n- Condition I is *not* a sufficient condition:\n\n$$\n({f}^{′}(x)=2x)\\,⇍\\,({f}^{″}(x)=2)\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nFor example, for the function $f(x)={x}^{2}+x,$ we have ${f}^{′}(x)=2x+1,$ and so the statement ${f}^{″}(x)=2$  is true but ${f}^{′}(x)\\ne 2x.$\n\n- Condition II is both necessary and sufficient: The statement ${f}^{′}(x)=2x$ is true *if and only if* $f(x)={x}^{2}+C,$ for any $C\\in R.$\n\n$$\n({f}^{′}(x)=2x)\\,⇒\\,(f(x)={x}^{2}+C)\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n({f}^{′}(x)=2x)\\,⇐\\,(f(x)={x}^{2}+C).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write\n\n$$\n({f}^{′}(x)=2x)\\,⇔\\,(f(x)={x}^{2}+C)\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\n- Condition III is *not* a necessary condition:\n\n$$\n({f}^{′}(x)=2x)\\,⇏\\,(f(x)={x}^{2})\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nFor example, for the function $f(x)={x}^{2}+1$ the statement ${f}^{′}(x)=2x$ is true but $f(x)\\ne {x}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420701 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186420677 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420701 #edb/ref 1069 "q-216328" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420701 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420701 #edb/ref 1071 "$\\,$ If $P$ and $Q$ are congruent triangles, then $P$ and $Q$ have the same area.\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the implication above?\n\n- $P$ and $Q$ being congruent triangles is a **necessary** condition for $P$ and $Q$ having the same area\n- $P$ and $Q$ having the same area is a **necessary** condition for $P$ and $Q$ being congruent triangles\n- $P$ and $Q$ being congruent triangles is a **sufficient** condition for $P$ and $Q$ having the same area\n- $P$ and $Q$ having the same area is a **sufficient** condition for $P$ and $Q$ being congruent triangles" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420701 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "bd31a8b1-49f0-5bbe-9bfb-4daa99eb8e90" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420701 #edb/ref 1074 "Given that the implication $S⇒N$ is true, we have the following:\n\n- $S$ being true *guarantees* that $N$ must also be true. Therefore, $S$ is a *sufficient condition* for $N.$\n\n- For $S$ to be true, it is *necessary* that $N$ is true. Therefore, $N$ is a *necessary condition* for $S.$ However, note that $N$ being true does *not* guarantee that $S$ is true.\n\nThese ideas can be summarized as follows:\n\n$$\n\\text{S}\\text{ufficient}\\,⇒\\,\\text{N}\\text{ecessary}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nIn our case, \n\n$$\n{{(\\,P\\text{ and }Q\\text{ are congruent triangles}\\,)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{sufficient}}\\,⇒\\,{{(\\,P\\text{ and }Q\\text{ have the same area}\\,)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{necessary}}\n$$\n\nis a true statement. Therefore:\n\n- Statement II is true. $P$ and $Q$ having the same area is a **necessary** condition for $P$ and $Q$ being congruent triangles.\n\n- Statement III is true. $P$ and $Q$ being congruent triangles is a **sufficient** condition for $P$ and $Q$ having the same area.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420702 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186420688 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420702 #edb/ref 1069 "q-172322" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420702 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420702 #edb/ref 1071 "$\\,$ $a\\ge 10$\n\nWhich of the following conditions are sufficient for the above to be true?\n\n- $a=10$\n- $a>15$\n- $a>5$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420702 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e4cdd56d-10e6-5abf-be98-585e1ce87428" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420702 #edb/ref 1074 "Given that the implication $S⇒N$ is true, we have the following:\n\n- $S$ being true *guarantees* that $N$ must also be true. Therefore, $S$ is a *sufficient condition* for $N.$\n\n- For $S$ to be true, it is *necessary* that $N$ is true. Therefore, $N$ is a *necessary condition* for $S.$ However, note that $N$ being true does *not* guarantee that $S$ is true.\n\nThese ideas can be summarized as follows:\n\n$$\n\\text{S}\\text{ufficient}\\,⇒\\,\\text{N}\\text{ecessary}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we need to find the conditions that are *sufficient* for the given one:\n\n$$\n\\text{S}\\text{ufficient}\\,⇒\\,(a\\ge 10)\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given conditions.\n\n- Condition I is a sufficient condition:\n\n$$\n(a=10)\\,⇒\\,(a\\ge 10)\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\nIf $a=10,$ then $a\\ge 10.$\n\n- Condition II is a sufficient condition:\n\n$$\n(a>15)\\,⇒\\,(a\\ge 10)\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\nIf $a>15,$ then $a\\ge 10.$\n\n- Condition III is *not* a sufficient condition:\n\n$$\n(a>5)\\,⇏\\,(a\\ge 10)\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nFor example, if $a=7,$ then we have $a≱10.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420703 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186420683 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420703 #edb/ref 1069 "q-216436" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420703 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420703 #edb/ref 1071 "$\\,$ ${x}^{2}+x-6\\le 0$\n\nWhich of the following conditions are necessary for the above to be true?\n\n- $x({x}^{2}+x-6)\\le 0$\n- $x\\le 2$\n- $x\\ge 0$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420703 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ed1378b7-d665-5e7d-b9bb-42ac28cb1db4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420703 #edb/ref 1074 "Given that the implication $S⇒N$ is true, we have the following:\n\n- $S$ being true *guarantees* that $N$ must also be true. Therefore, $S$ is a *sufficient condition* for $N.$\n\n- For $S$ to be true, it is *necessary* that $N$ is true. Therefore, $N$ is a *necessary condition* for $S.$ However, note that $N$ being true does *not* guarantee that $S$ is true.\n\nThese ideas can be summarized as follows:\n\n$$\n\\text{S}\\text{ufficient}\\,⇒\\,\\text{N}\\text{ecessary}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we need to find the conditions that are *necessary* from the given one:\n\n$$\n({x}^{2}+x-6\\le 0)\\,⇒\\,\\text{N}\\text{ecessary}\n$$\n\nNote that the solution to the given inequality is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+x-6 & \\le 0 \\\\ (x+3)(x-2) & \\le 0 \\\\ -3\\le x\\le 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given conditions.\n\n- Condition I is *not* a necessary condition:\n\n$$\n({x}^{2}+x-6\\le 0)\\,⇏\\,(x({x}^{2}+x-6)\\le 0)\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nFor example, if $x=-1,$ then ${x}^{2}+x-6=-6\\le 0,$ but $x({x}^{2}+x-6)=6≰0.$\n\n- Condition II is a necessary condition:\n\n$$\n({x}^{2}+x-6\\le 0)\\,⇒\\,(x\\le 2)\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\nEvery solution of ${x}^{2}+x-6\\le 0$ satisfies $x\\le 2.$\n\n- Condition III is *not* a necessary condition:\n\n$$\n({x}^{2}+x-6\\le 0)\\,⇏\\,(x\\ge 0)\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nFor example, if $x=-1,$ then ${x}^{2}+x-6=-6\\le 0,$ but $x≱0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420704 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186420676 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420704 #edb/ref 1069 "q-216447" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420704 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420704 #edb/ref 1071 "$\\,$ $18∣x$\n\nWhich of the following conditions are necessary and sufficient for the above statement to be true?\n\n- $2∣x$ and $9∣x$\n- $3∣x$ and $6∣x$\n- $72∣x$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420704 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f653d451-6beb-59b1-affd-6d539e9553ca" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420704 #edb/ref 1074 "Given that the equivalence $P⇔Q$ is true, we have that $P$ is a *necessary and sufficient condition* for $Q.$  \n\nIn our case, we need to find the conditions that are *necessary and sufficient* for the given one. In other words,\n\n$$\n(18∣x)\\,⇒\\,\\text{Condition}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n(18∣x)\\,⇐\\,\\text{Condition}.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given conditions.\n\n- Condition I is both necessary and sufficient: A number is divisible by $18$ *if and only if* it's divisible by both $2$ and $9.$\n\n$$\n(18∣x)\\,⇒\\,(2∣x\\text{ and }9∣x)\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n(18∣x)\\,⇐\\,(2∣x\\text{ and }9∣x).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write\n\n$$\n(18∣x)\\,⇔\\,(2∣x\\text{ and }9∣x)\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\n- Condition II is *not* a sufficient condition:\n\n$$\n(18∣x)\\,⇍\\,(3∣x\\text{ and }6∣x)\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nFor example, the number $x=6$ is divisible by $3$ and by $6$ but not divisible by $18.$\n\n- Condition III is *not* a necessary condition:\n\n$$\n(18∣x)\\,⇏\\,(72∣x)\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nFor example, the number $x=18$ is divisible by $18$ but not divisible by $72.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420705 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186420686 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420705 #edb/ref 1069 "q-183396" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420705 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420705 #edb/ref 1071 "$\\,$ If $A⊂B,$ then $A\\cap \\overset{―}{B}=∅.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the implication above?\n\n- $A\\cap \\overset{―}{B}=∅$ is a **necessary** condition for $A⊂B$\n- $A⊂B$ is a **necessary** condition for $A\\cap \\overset{―}{B}=∅$\n- $A\\cap \\overset{―}{B}=∅$ is a **sufficient** condition for $A⊂B$\n- $A⊂B$ is a **sufficient** condition for $A\\cap \\overset{―}{B}=∅$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420705 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fb9b0e1b-3e64-5482-b003-6352af7b5cae" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420705 #edb/ref 1074 "Given that the implication $S⇒N$ is true, we have the following:\n\n- $S$ being true *guarantees* that $N$ must also be true. Therefore, $S$ is a *sufficient condition* for $N.$\n\n- For $S$ to be true, it is *necessary* that $N$ is true. Therefore, $N$ is a *necessary condition* for $S.$ However, note that $N$ being true does *not* guarantee that $S$ is true.\n\nThese ideas can be summarized as follows:\n\n$$\n\\text{S}\\text{ufficient}\\,⇒\\,\\text{N}\\text{ecessary}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nIn our case, \n\n$$\n{{(\\,A⊂B\\,)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{sufficient}}\\,⇒\\,{{(\\,A\\cap \\overset{―}{B}=∅\\,)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{necessary}}\n$$\n\nis a true statement. Therefore:\n\n- Statement I is true. $A\\cap \\overset{―}{B}=∅$ is a **necessary** condition for $A⊂B.$\n\n- Statement IV is true. $A⊂B$ is a **sufficient** condition for $A\\cap \\overset{―}{B}=∅.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and IV only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
